{
  "abstract": "Finite polynomial approximation in quadratic-jet determinant estimates must retain enough dimension and also achieve exponential residual depth. We quantify the distinction for uniform Hahn projection of complex geometric sequences. A Laguerre comparison gives a complete finite Chernoff rank bound and a uniform exponential spectral law whenever $H(1-|z|^2)$ tends to infinity, with an explicit error valid across all phases. A finite amplitude estimate gives the complementary hyperbolic-sine profile for radial perturbations of size $1/H$. For real arguments, a classical coefficient saddle identifies the exact exponential-moment threshold. It agrees with the weak-limit rate up to $z=2-\\sqrt3$ and becomes strictly smaller above that radius; finite coefficient estimates justify convergence below the threshold and divergence at it. Critical growth is of order $H^{1/2}$ except at the boundary, where it is $H^{2/3}$, with explicit constants and a joint scaling limit. Retaining coefficient-circle width then yields a complete complex residual bound whose nonsingular prefactor is independent of grid size at fixed proportional rank and positive decay slope. A new 7,808-cell slope cover transfers this bound to the unchanged forty-bin quadratic-jet source, reducing its trace log-prefactor from approximately 122.80535 to 45.83847. The metric correction exceeds 0.0030166950513699, below the existing endpoint-weight refinement. Classical Laguerre, Hahn and large-power asymptotic results are credited. Exact algebra, directed CPU replay and explicit finite constants support the author arguments; earlier rate-depth and arithmetic certificates are imported rather than rerun. No new discriminant endpoint, census, classification or all-scale source theorem is claimed. The imported $D\\le498$ consequence retains its $D\\le499$ qualification.",
